  "scenes": [
    {
      "narration": "Having a baby carries a price tag that varies enormously by country, mostly because of what the government does, or doesn't, do.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "hook",
          "art": "home",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "home"
          ],
          "lines": [
            "A baby,",
            "5 countries."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s1"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Just giving birth in the US can cost around three thousand dollars out of pocket, even with insurance.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "home",
          "big": "$3,000",
          "label": "US out-of-pocket to give birth",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "x"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "narration": "In most peer countries, the birth itself costs families almost nothing.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-costraise",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · cost of raising a child"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s3"
    },
    {
      "narration": "And then there's time. The US guarantees zero weeks of federal paid parental leave.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "home",
          "big": "0 weeks",
          "label": "US paid leave guarantee",
          "props": [
            "x",
            "clock"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s4"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Most rich countries guarantee months of paid parental leave.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-leave",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · parental leave"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s5"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Many countries also mail parents a monthly child benefit. The US mostly does not.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "home",
          "big": "~$2K/yr",
          "label": "child benefit, that US skips",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "x"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A child benefit is regular cash support per child.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-benefit",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · child benefit"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s7"
    },
    {
      "narration": "All in, raising one US child to eighteen costs around three hundred thousand dollars, before college.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "home",
          "big": "$300K",
          "label": "to raise a US child to 18",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "fire"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s8"
    },
    {
      "narration": "In the US, a baby is a private expense; in much of the world, it's treated as a shared one.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "Private vs shared",
          "props": [
            "home",
            "coin"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s9"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A welfare state spreads those early costs across everyone.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-welfare",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · welfare state"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s10"
    },
    {
